1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,810
Meet Hiro.

2
00:00:02,150 --> 00:00:03,441
He makes small,

3
00:00:03,441 --> 00:00:06,075
precise metal parts in a workshop in

4
00:00:06,075 --> 00:00:08,156
a mountain town in Japan,

5
00:00:08,156 --> 00:00:10,263
parts that end up inside cars and

6
00:00:10,263 --> 00:00:12,970
machines all over the world.

7
00:00:13,130 --> 00:00:14,551
For a long time now,

8
00:00:14,551 --> 00:00:16,478
the same paper sign has hung in

9
00:00:16,478 --> 00:00:17,800
his window.

10
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:19,290
Help wanted.

11
00:00:19,290 --> 00:00:21,010
Nobody calls.

12
00:00:21,370 --> 00:00:23,100
Hiro isn't unlucky.

13
00:00:23,260 --> 00:00:25,185
His whole country is running out of

14
00:00:25,185 --> 00:00:26,170
workers.

15
00:00:26,330 --> 00:00:27,081
And yet,

16
00:00:27,081 --> 00:00:29,128
a short drive from towns like his,

17
00:00:29,128 --> 00:00:31,978
Japan builds almost four in every ten

18
00:00:31,978 --> 00:00:34,650
industrial robots made on Earth.

19
00:00:34,810 --> 00:00:37,044
So could the world's oldest big country

20
00:00:37,044 --> 00:00:39,775
turn its biggest problem into its biggest

21
00:00:39,775 --> 00:00:41,033
advantage?

22
00:00:41,483 --> 00:00:48,400
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

23
00:00:59,850 --> 00:01:02,167
Hoshimachi isn't a real town,

24
00:01:02,167 --> 00:01:04,249
but towns like it are all over

25
00:01:04,249 --> 00:01:05,370
Japan.

26
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:07,560
The train comes less often.

27
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:10,150
More shops keep their shutters down.

28
00:01:10,350 --> 00:01:12,841
Hiro's apprentice left for the city years

29
00:01:12,841 --> 00:01:13,498
ago,

30
00:01:13,498 --> 00:01:15,084
and his stool still sits by the

31
00:01:15,084 --> 00:01:15,910
lathe.

32
00:01:16,050 --> 00:01:17,069
The strange thing is,

33
00:01:17,069 --> 00:01:19,460
Hiro's customers aren't leaving.

34
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:21,450
They want more parts than ever.

35
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:24,000
What's missing is hands.

36
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:26,510
Let me draw what happened.

37
00:01:26,510 --> 00:01:28,554
This bar is everyone of working age

38
00:01:28,554 --> 00:01:29,352
in Japan,

39
00:01:29,352 --> 00:01:30,149
at the peak,

40
00:01:30,149 --> 00:01:32,150
a generation ago.

41
00:01:32,150 --> 00:01:34,050
This bar is today.

42
00:01:34,050 --> 00:01:34,996
Since that peak,

43
00:01:34,996 --> 00:01:37,420
Japan has lost about thirteen and a

44
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:39,960
half million working-age people.

45
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:42,107
That's almost as many people as live

46
00:01:42,107 --> 00:01:43,780
in all of Tokyo.

47
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:46,663
Picture every office and every train in

48
00:01:46,663 --> 00:01:47,289
Tokyo,

49
00:01:47,289 --> 00:01:48,290
empty.

50
00:01:48,290 --> 00:01:50,227
That's the size of the hole.

51
00:01:50,484 --> 00:01:52,784
And it isn't slowing down.

52
00:01:52,784 --> 00:01:54,770
The government's own population

53
00:01:54,770 --> 00:01:56,293
researchers project that,

54
00:01:56,293 --> 00:01:58,920
within the lifetime of today's children,

55
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:01,127
Japan will have only about half the

56
00:02:01,127 --> 00:02:03,934
working-age people it had at the peak.

57
00:02:04,194 --> 00:02:06,299
Think of a rush-hour train platform,

58
00:02:06,299 --> 00:02:08,554
where you can barely move.

59
00:02:08,624 --> 00:02:11,622
Now take away every other person.

60
00:02:11,884 --> 00:02:13,603
You can feel it in the job

61
00:02:13,603 --> 00:02:14,574
market.

62
00:02:14,574 --> 00:02:15,783
Month after month,

63
00:02:15,783 --> 00:02:18,004
Japan has had more open jobs than

64
00:02:18,004 --> 00:02:20,084
people looking for work.

65
00:02:20,174 --> 00:02:22,340
Hiro is competing for workers with the

66
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:23,304
convenience store,

67
00:02:23,304 --> 00:02:26,444
the nursing home and the delivery company.

68
00:02:26,444 --> 00:02:28,626
And they're all losing too.

69
00:02:33,084 --> 00:02:36,094
Take the trucks that carry Hiro's parts.

70
00:02:36,094 --> 00:02:38,800
Japan capped truck drivers' overtime to

71
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:40,384
protect their health.

72
00:02:40,404 --> 00:02:42,104
Good for the drivers.

73
00:02:42,104 --> 00:02:43,568
But fewer drivers,

74
00:02:43,568 --> 00:02:45,033
working fewer hours,

75
00:02:45,033 --> 00:02:46,874
move fewer boxes.

76
00:02:47,004 --> 00:02:48,464
The government warns that,

77
00:02:48,464 --> 00:02:49,712
if nothing changes,

78
00:02:49,712 --> 00:02:51,616
by the end of the decade Japan

79
00:02:51,616 --> 00:02:53,308
could be short of trucks for about

80
00:02:53,308 --> 00:02:55,178
a third of its cargo.

81
00:02:55,417 --> 00:02:57,877
Then there's Hiro's mother.

82
00:02:58,077 --> 00:02:59,437
She lives in a care home down

83
00:02:59,437 --> 00:03:00,377
the road.

84
00:03:00,487 --> 00:03:03,217
It needs more caregivers every year,

85
00:03:03,217 --> 00:03:05,511
because more people grow old and fewer

86
00:03:05,511 --> 00:03:07,717
young people arrive to help.

87
00:03:08,047 --> 00:03:10,556
The health ministry's own estimate is that

88
00:03:10,556 --> 00:03:12,837
Japan will need hundreds of thousands more

89
00:03:12,837 --> 00:03:15,490
caregivers in the years ahead.

90
00:03:15,750 --> 00:03:17,536
Even Hiro's granddaughter,

91
00:03:17,536 --> 00:03:17,966
Aoi,

92
00:03:17,966 --> 00:03:19,470
moved away.

93
00:03:19,510 --> 00:03:22,220
She writes software in Tokyo now.

94
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:24,190
Hiro is proud of her.

95
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:26,106
He still needs someone to run the

96
00:03:26,106 --> 00:03:26,941
lathe.

97
00:03:27,184 --> 00:03:27,987
So far,

98
00:03:27,987 --> 00:03:30,756
this sounds like the story everyone tells

99
00:03:30,756 --> 00:03:32,464
about Japan.

100
00:03:32,534 --> 00:03:33,016
Old,

101
00:03:33,016 --> 00:03:33,751
shrinking,

102
00:03:33,751 --> 00:03:34,344
stuck.

103
00:03:34,344 --> 00:03:37,154
But here's the twist.

104
00:03:37,194 --> 00:03:39,220
The same kind of shortage that is

105
00:03:39,220 --> 00:03:42,006
emptying Hoshimachi helped create one of

106
00:03:42,006 --> 00:03:44,378
Japan's greatest industries.

107
00:03:48,817 --> 00:03:51,707
Go back to nineteen sixty-nine.

108
00:03:51,707 --> 00:03:54,820
Japan's economy was booming and factories

109
00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:56,617
couldn't hire fast enough.

110
00:03:56,687 --> 00:03:57,705
Kawasaki,

111
00:03:57,705 --> 00:03:59,554
a heavy-industry company,

112
00:03:59,554 --> 00:04:01,839
began building the first industrial robot

113
00:04:01,839 --> 00:04:02,857
made in Japan,

114
00:04:02,857 --> 00:04:05,327
under an American license.

115
00:04:05,417 --> 00:04:08,191
Carmakers hungry for workers were among

116
00:04:08,191 --> 00:04:09,547
the first buyers.

117
00:04:09,784 --> 00:04:10,394
Today,

118
00:04:10,394 --> 00:04:13,338
the International Federation of Robotics

119
00:04:13,338 --> 00:04:15,652
calls Japan the world's predominant robot

120
00:04:15,652 --> 00:04:17,724
manufacturing country.

121
00:04:18,104 --> 00:04:18,839
FANUC,

122
00:04:18,839 --> 00:04:19,828
Yaskawa,

123
00:04:19,828 --> 00:04:22,616
Kawasaki and others build the arms that

124
00:04:22,616 --> 00:04:23,351
weld cars,

125
00:04:23,351 --> 00:04:26,393
assemble electronics and lift boxes on

126
00:04:26,393 --> 00:04:28,514
every continent.

127
00:04:28,654 --> 00:04:30,679
These are industrial robots,

128
00:04:30,679 --> 00:04:32,154
the workhorses.

129
00:04:32,154 --> 00:04:33,102
They don't walk,

130
00:04:33,102 --> 00:04:34,604
and they don't chat.

131
00:04:34,604 --> 00:04:36,173
They just work.

132
00:04:36,417 --> 00:04:38,877
But here's the strange part.

133
00:04:39,107 --> 00:04:41,437
Look at where the robots go.

134
00:04:41,677 --> 00:04:43,698
In its latest fiscal year,

135
00:04:43,698 --> 00:04:46,512
nearly nine of every ten dollars of

136
00:04:46,512 --> 00:04:49,325
FANUC's sales came from customers outside

137
00:04:49,325 --> 00:04:50,437
Japan.

138
00:04:50,887 --> 00:04:53,457
Its robot sales in China jumped.

139
00:04:53,457 --> 00:04:56,187
Its robot sales at home fell.

140
00:04:56,507 --> 00:04:58,397
Japan builds the robots.

141
00:04:58,397 --> 00:05:00,213
The world buys them.

142
00:05:00,450 --> 00:05:03,530
So who actually uses the most robots?

143
00:05:03,530 --> 00:05:05,763
Count robots per factory worker,

144
00:05:05,763 --> 00:05:07,567
and South Korea has more than twice

145
00:05:07,567 --> 00:05:09,520
as many as Japan.

146
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:11,964
Singapore and Germany are ahead of Japan

147
00:05:11,964 --> 00:05:12,750
too.

148
00:05:12,770 --> 00:05:15,324
The robot superpower is not the most

149
00:05:15,324 --> 00:05:17,085
robotic country.

150
00:05:17,350 --> 00:05:19,597
Why would a shortage be good news

151
00:05:19,597 --> 00:05:21,040
for robots?

152
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:22,460
Let me draw it.

153
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:25,388
Fewer workers means employers compete for

154
00:05:25,388 --> 00:05:25,911
them,

155
00:05:25,911 --> 00:05:27,610
so wages rise.

156
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:29,807
Higher wages make a robot look cheap

157
00:05:29,807 --> 00:05:31,470
by comparison.

158
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:33,429
So companies buy robots,

159
00:05:33,429 --> 00:05:37,040
and every remaining worker produces more.

160
00:05:37,290 --> 00:05:40,220
That last box is the whole game.

161
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:42,000
Output per worker.

162
00:05:42,110 --> 00:05:44,441
A country can shrink and still get

163
00:05:44,441 --> 00:05:46,160
richer per person,

164
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:48,799
if each pair of hands does more.

165
00:05:53,250 --> 00:05:55,259
Step behind the drink fridge in a

166
00:05:55,259 --> 00:05:56,991
Tokyo convenience store,

167
00:05:56,991 --> 00:05:58,999
and you may find a robot arm

168
00:05:58,999 --> 00:06:02,110
sliding cold bottles onto the shelves.

169
00:06:02,370 --> 00:06:03,313
Its maker,

170
00:06:03,313 --> 00:06:05,706
a startup called Telexistence,

171
00:06:05,706 --> 00:06:07,881
says one arm can restock about a

172
00:06:07,881 --> 00:06:09,690
thousand drinks a day.

173
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:11,589
When it fumbles a bottle,

174
00:06:11,589 --> 00:06:13,576
a person somewhere else puts on a

175
00:06:13,576 --> 00:06:16,560
headset and finishes the job remotely.

176
00:06:16,780 --> 00:06:18,310
That's not a demo.

177
00:06:18,370 --> 00:06:20,494
That's a deployment.

178
00:06:20,750 --> 00:06:23,055
Now compare that with the robots in

179
00:06:23,055 --> 00:06:24,680
the headlines.

180
00:06:24,710 --> 00:06:27,401
Japan's most famous walking humanoid,

181
00:06:27,401 --> 00:06:28,857
Honda's ASIMO,

182
00:06:28,857 --> 00:06:31,078
charmed crowds for years and retired

183
00:06:31,078 --> 00:06:33,900
without ever getting a real job.

184
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:34,706
Today,

185
00:06:34,706 --> 00:06:37,781
Seven-Eleven Japan and Telexistence are

186
00:06:37,781 --> 00:06:40,359
developing a humanoid helper,

187
00:06:40,359 --> 00:06:42,488
with a target of putting it in

188
00:06:42,488 --> 00:06:44,710
stores by twenty twenty-nine.

189
00:06:44,710 --> 00:06:45,120
That's a plan,

190
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:46,140
not a product.

191
00:06:46,250 --> 00:06:47,330
In Japan,

192
00:06:47,330 --> 00:06:49,545
the robots doing the work right now

193
00:06:49,545 --> 00:06:50,348
are arms,

194
00:06:50,348 --> 00:06:51,778
not androids.

195
00:06:52,017 --> 00:06:53,757
Back at the care home,

196
00:06:53,757 --> 00:06:56,127
the helpers are quieter.

197
00:06:56,167 --> 00:06:58,380
Sensors under the mattress that tell night

198
00:06:58,380 --> 00:07:00,087
staff who is awake.

199
00:07:00,227 --> 00:07:02,509
Machines that help lift residents out of

200
00:07:02,509 --> 00:07:03,317
bed.

201
00:07:03,547 --> 00:07:06,038
Economists who studied Japanese nursing

202
00:07:06,038 --> 00:07:08,507
homes found something surprising.

203
00:07:08,717 --> 00:07:11,622
Homes that adopted robots ended up with

204
00:07:11,622 --> 00:07:12,324
more staff,

205
00:07:12,324 --> 00:07:13,268
not fewer,

206
00:07:13,268 --> 00:07:15,689
and caregivers spent more of their time

207
00:07:15,689 --> 00:07:18,710
on what the researchers call human touch.

208
00:07:18,950 --> 00:07:21,650
Which brings us back to Hiro.

209
00:07:21,650 --> 00:07:22,544
One weekend,

210
00:07:22,544 --> 00:07:24,670
Aoi comes home with a plan.

211
00:07:24,670 --> 00:07:26,040
Not a humanoid.

212
00:07:26,040 --> 00:07:28,567
A small collaborative robot arm,

213
00:07:28,567 --> 00:07:30,886
the kind built to work safely beside

214
00:07:30,886 --> 00:07:31,950
people.

215
00:07:32,050 --> 00:07:33,420
They name it Kobo,

216
00:07:33,420 --> 00:07:36,110
from the Japanese word for workshop.

217
00:07:36,180 --> 00:07:38,250
Aoi writes the program.

218
00:07:38,250 --> 00:07:39,611
Kobo loads the lathe,

219
00:07:39,611 --> 00:07:40,971
again and again,

220
00:07:40,971 --> 00:07:43,278
while Hiro does the part only he

221
00:07:43,278 --> 00:07:43,946
can do,

222
00:07:43,946 --> 00:07:46,714
checking every piece with a lifetime of

223
00:07:46,714 --> 00:07:48,006
experience.

224
00:07:48,250 --> 00:07:50,860
But who gets paid for Kobo?

225
00:07:50,860 --> 00:07:52,320
Let me draw it.

226
00:07:52,350 --> 00:07:52,840
First,

227
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:53,587
the arm,

228
00:07:53,587 --> 00:07:54,960
the hardware.

229
00:07:54,970 --> 00:07:57,070
Japan is superb at this.

230
00:07:57,070 --> 00:07:57,872
Second,

231
00:07:57,872 --> 00:07:58,673
the brain,

232
00:07:58,673 --> 00:08:01,161
the AI software that lets a robot

233
00:08:01,161 --> 00:08:02,590
see and learn.

234
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:04,704
Much of that is written in America

235
00:08:04,704 --> 00:08:05,850
and China.

236
00:08:05,890 --> 00:08:06,927
And finally,

237
00:08:06,927 --> 00:08:07,752
the user,

238
00:08:07,752 --> 00:08:09,867
the small business that puts the robot

239
00:08:09,867 --> 00:08:10,890
to work.

240
00:08:11,040 --> 00:08:12,600
In an AI economy,

241
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:14,180
more and more of the money flows

242
00:08:14,180 --> 00:08:16,360
to the brain and to the user.

243
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:18,789
Japan owns the arm.

244
00:08:23,250 --> 00:08:25,170
Now the catches.

245
00:08:25,170 --> 00:08:25,312
First,

246
00:08:25,312 --> 00:08:25,890
the currency.

247
00:08:25,890 --> 00:08:27,399
Since the pandemic,

248
00:08:27,399 --> 00:08:29,702
Japan's money has lost more than a

249
00:08:29,702 --> 00:08:32,810
quarter of its value against the dollar.

250
00:08:32,810 --> 00:08:35,263
That makes Japanese robots cheap for

251
00:08:35,263 --> 00:08:36,219
foreign buyers,

252
00:08:36,219 --> 00:08:37,800
which helps FANUC.

253
00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:40,407
But it makes every imported chip,

254
00:08:40,407 --> 00:08:43,123
every barrel of fuel and every software

255
00:08:43,123 --> 00:08:45,813
subscription more expensive for Hiro.

256
00:08:46,050 --> 00:08:47,009
Second,

257
00:08:47,009 --> 00:08:48,170
software.

258
00:08:48,170 --> 00:08:50,303
Japan builds superb machines,

259
00:08:50,303 --> 00:08:52,940
but the economy around them hasn't kept

260
00:08:52,940 --> 00:08:53,780
up.

261
00:08:54,050 --> 00:08:56,475
Measured by output per hour worked,

262
00:08:56,475 --> 00:08:58,478
Japan sits in the bottom third of

263
00:08:58,478 --> 00:09:00,190
the rich-country club.

264
00:09:00,350 --> 00:09:03,050
And remember FANUC's robot sales at home?

265
00:09:03,050 --> 00:09:04,280
They fell.

266
00:09:04,310 --> 00:09:06,500
The robots are being made here.

267
00:09:06,510 --> 00:09:08,406
They just aren't spreading fast enough

268
00:09:08,406 --> 00:09:09,216
here.

269
00:09:09,450 --> 00:09:11,630
And then there is debt.

270
00:09:11,640 --> 00:09:14,332
The IMF says Japan's government owes about

271
00:09:14,332 --> 00:09:17,002
twice the size of its entire economy,

272
00:09:17,002 --> 00:09:20,070
the highest of any advanced country.

273
00:09:20,150 --> 00:09:22,792
The government also owns large financial

274
00:09:22,792 --> 00:09:23,488
assets,

275
00:09:23,488 --> 00:09:26,050
so the net picture is less extreme.

276
00:09:26,050 --> 00:09:27,978
But as more people retire,

277
00:09:27,978 --> 00:09:30,174
more money goes to pensions and health

278
00:09:30,174 --> 00:09:30,638
care,

279
00:09:30,638 --> 00:09:33,922
and there's less room for everything else.

280
00:09:34,184 --> 00:09:35,558
Add it all up,

281
00:09:35,558 --> 00:09:37,800
and you can see why so many

282
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:41,374
videos call Japan a slow-motion collapse.

283
00:09:41,374 --> 00:09:42,068
Last year,

284
00:09:42,068 --> 00:09:45,177
its whole economy grew barely more than

285
00:09:45,177 --> 00:09:46,374
one percent.

286
00:09:46,374 --> 00:09:47,149
And yet,

287
00:09:47,149 --> 00:09:49,823
that headline may be measuring the wrong

288
00:09:49,823 --> 00:09:50,690
thing.

289
00:09:55,150 --> 00:09:56,653
Economists led by Jesús

290
00:09:56,653 --> 00:09:59,444
Fernández-Villaverde of the University of

291
00:09:59,444 --> 00:10:02,620
Pennsylvania asked a different question.

292
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:05,585
Not how fast Japan grew per citizen,

293
00:10:05,585 --> 00:10:07,870
but per working-age adult.

294
00:10:08,010 --> 00:10:09,180
Their answer?

295
00:10:09,320 --> 00:10:12,004
From the late nineties until the pandemic,

296
00:10:12,004 --> 00:10:15,100
Japan grew slightly faster than America.

297
00:10:15,350 --> 00:10:17,220
Let me draw it.

298
00:10:17,220 --> 00:10:19,589
Japan's total economy looks flat,

299
00:10:19,589 --> 00:10:21,545
because the number of workers keeps

300
00:10:21,545 --> 00:10:22,580
falling.

301
00:10:22,710 --> 00:10:25,132
But divide by the people who actually

302
00:10:25,132 --> 00:10:25,550
work,

303
00:10:25,550 --> 00:10:26,629
and the line climbs,

304
00:10:26,629 --> 00:10:28,990
right alongside America's.

305
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:32,220
Japan's problem was never lazy workers.

306
00:10:32,220 --> 00:10:33,960
It was fewer of them.

307
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:36,709
And fewer hands is exactly the problem

308
00:10:36,709 --> 00:10:38,742
robots are built to solve.

309
00:10:38,984 --> 00:10:39,560
So,

310
00:10:39,560 --> 00:10:42,584
will AI make Japan rich again?

311
00:10:42,634 --> 00:10:44,166
Not the way it was in the

312
00:10:44,166 --> 00:10:44,801
bubble,

313
00:10:44,801 --> 00:10:46,552
when Tokyo land was said to be

314
00:10:46,552 --> 00:10:48,684
worth more than entire countries.

315
00:10:48,794 --> 00:10:50,880
That boom was built on debt and

316
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:52,194
land prices.

317
00:10:52,194 --> 00:10:52,846
This one,

318
00:10:52,846 --> 00:10:53,947
if it comes,

319
00:10:53,947 --> 00:10:55,745
would be built on output per pair

320
00:10:55,745 --> 00:10:56,614
of hands.

321
00:10:56,614 --> 00:10:58,826
Robots and AI can let a shrinking

322
00:10:58,826 --> 00:11:01,884
Japan keep getting richer per person.

323
00:11:01,894 --> 00:11:04,100
But only if small firms like Hiro's

324
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:05,547
actually use them,

325
00:11:05,547 --> 00:11:07,753
Japan writes more of the brains and

326
00:11:07,753 --> 00:11:08,954
not just the arms,

327
00:11:08,954 --> 00:11:11,406
and the debt doesn't crowd everything else

328
00:11:11,406 --> 00:11:12,251
out.

329
00:11:12,517 --> 00:11:13,945
In Hoshimachi,

330
00:11:13,945 --> 00:11:16,123
the help-wanted sign is still in the

331
00:11:16,123 --> 00:11:17,207
window.

332
00:11:17,377 --> 00:11:19,817
Hiro still wants an apprentice.

333
00:11:19,947 --> 00:11:22,132
But now the parts ship on time,

334
00:11:22,132 --> 00:11:24,621
and Aoi comes home to teach Kobo

335
00:11:24,621 --> 00:11:26,327
something new.

336
00:11:26,697 --> 00:11:30,037
Japan's biggest problem isn't going away.

337
00:11:30,217 --> 00:11:32,969
What's changing is what a workshop with

338
00:11:32,969 --> 00:11:35,098
too few hands can do.

339
00:11:35,350 --> 00:11:36,055
Next time,

340
00:11:36,055 --> 00:11:39,215
we follow the money behind another hidden

341
00:11:39,215 --> 00:11:41,760
link of the world economy.

342
00:11:41,880 --> 00:11:44,492
This is The Hidden Economics.
